mpc2000xl: tune desktop DATA wheel keys, drag and scroll

F14/F15 made the DATA wheel unreachable on an ordinary ANSI keyboard. F9
and F10 are not substitutes here: this same matrix already assigns them to
16 Levels and Next Seq. Put decrement/increment on minus/equals, which are
free and adjacent on that keyboard.

A relative analog field applies PORT_KEYDELTA on every input frame while a
digital direction is held, then applies PORT_SENSITIVITY to the accumulated
field. The original sensitivity 100/key delta 1 therefore cannot make both a
short key tap exactly one detent and a hold repeat slowly. Keep mouse motion
on the low byte, and put the two keyboard directions on spare high bits. The
existing 60 Hz panel dial timer gives an edge one detent, waits 300 ms, then
repeats every six ticks (10 detents/second). Add the keyboard offset to both
the accurate panel and optional panel-HLE reads, and save it with the machine.

Use sensitivity 20 so a slow 100-pixel vertical drag gives 20 detents. Keep
scroll on the analog field's positive/negative switch sequences: SDL exposes
one wheel notch as a mouse-Z switch, and key delta 3 at 20 percent resolves to
one analog detent per notch in both directions.

The artwork explained the old one-click drag limit. Its raw input tag made
render_target::update_pointer_fields() call set_value(1) while the marker was
held, overriding relative input. The layout helper then wrote user_value, but
ioport_field::set_user_settings() only applies that value to adjusters and
setting lists, not analog dials. Remove both broken paths and use IPT_DIAL_V,
whose standard assignment is the window-wide mouse-Y relative axis.

Measured on the MAIN screen Sq field with a private Xvfb, LCD export, private
configuration, and the same focused build. Before this amendment, a 50 ms
keydown/keyup moved Sq:01 to Sq:16 (15 steps); a scroll notch moved exactly
one; and a slow 100-pixel drag changed the raw dial by 100 and drove Sq:01 to
its Sq:99 limit (98 visible steps before saturation). After tuning, an exact
50 ms emulated key press moves one step; a two-second hold moves 17 steps in
total (the edge plus steady 10/second repeat after 300 ms); each scroll notch
moves one step; and a slow 100-pixel drag moves exactly 20 LCD steps. All four
after values were captured in one run of the same binary.

A binding-table collision check also found that A drove both Step < and Undo,
and S drove both Step > and Erase. Keep A/S on the step pair and move the two
editing controls to the previously unused Backspace/Delete keys so every key
has one unambiguous panel action.
